ng a guide and get stuck on this problem, make sure you have done the pre-r=
eqs.</p>
<ol>
<li data-length=3D"1">
<p>Understand recursion. Don't just skip to this, otherwise understanding t=
he call stack will be difficult. Do basic recursion problems. Do tree probl=
ems. The basis of backtracking lies in traversing N-ary trees.</p>
</li>
<li data-length=3D"1">
<p>Draw out the state diagram. In this problem, you have two decisions you =
can make. Pick the number at the current index, or skip that number. Drawin=
g state diagrams can be time consuming but it is important.</p>
</li>
<li data-length=3D"1">
<p>The loop allows you to define all available options for each recursive c=
all, but don't just blindly memorize it. Begin with understanding the solut=
ion that purely involves the two function calls and you'll begin to see why=
 its used.</p>
</li>
<li data-length=3D"1">
<p>Once you've truly understood this problem, move on to Combination Sum an=
d other backtracking problems that involve pick or skip.</p>
</li>
<li data-length=3D"1">
<p>If you've made it here, utilize DFS + For Loops. You will need to use th=
is concept, and an immediate application from here is Letter Combinations o=
f a Phone Number. This will be also be valuable for DFS grid questions, thi=
nk No. Islands, Word Search.</p>
</li>
<li data-length=3D"1">
<p>Most backtracking questions are just combinations, pick/skip, and permut=
ations with extra steps. Focus on applying the pattern and learn how to pru=
ne branches. Pruning can involve skipping steps or discarding the entire ca=
ll stack.</p>
</li>
<li data-length=3D"1">
<p>To start with DP, understand how to brute-force, then top-down memo, the=
n bottom up. There's a great guide <a href=3D"https://leetcode.com/link/?ta=
rget=3Dhttps%3A%2F%2Fleetcode.com%2Fproblems%2Fhouse-robber%2Fsolutions%2F1=
56523%2Ffrom-good-to-great-how-to-approach-most-of-dp-problems%2F" target=
=3D"_blank">here</a></p>
</li>
